text:
"Let him be slain!" "Let him be burned!" "Let him be eaten!" — so the enraged rākṣasas there said to one another.
Swiftly traversing the path, that great-souled one arrived before the feet of the lord of rākṣasas and beheld there the senior attendants of the king and the palace adorned with great jewels.
The immensely resplendent Rāvaṇa saw that foremost of monkeys being dragged hither and thither by rākṣasas of hideous forms.
And that foremost of monkeys also beheld the lord of rākṣasas, endowed with blazing power and strength, blazing like the sun.
Ten-faced Rāvaṇa, his gaze reddened with swelling wrath, scrutinized the monkey and then directed the chief counselors seated there — elders distinguished by lineage, conduct, and age — regarding him.
Questioned by them in due order as to the purpose of his business and the root cause of the matter, the monkey announced at the outset: "I have come from the presence of Sugrīva, lord of the monkeys, as his messenger."
Then Hanūmān of terrible valor, astonished by his deeds, his eyes reddened with wrath, gazed upon the lord of rākṣasas.
He beheld him resplendent with a magnificent golden diadem wrapped in a net of pearls, of surpassing brilliance —
— adorned with golden ornaments of manifold kinds inset with highly precious gems set with diamonds, fashioned as if by the mind itself;
— clad in the most costly silk, smeared with red sandal paste, self-anointed, with varied and diverse decorative patterns;
— with broad, striking rākṣasa eyes terrible to behold, with blazing, sharp, and great fangs, and with hanging lips over the teeth;
— that hero gleaming with great splendor, with his ten heads, like Mount Mandara covered with various serpents at its peaks;
— dark as a heap of collyrium, his necklace gleaming on his chest, his face like the full moon, like a cloud attended by cranes;

— his arms bound with armlets and smeared with the finest sandal paste, adorned with gleaming bracelets, massive, like five-headed serpents;
— seated on a great, jeweled, variegated crystal throne adorned with the finest gems, spread with the best of cushions;
— attended on all sides by exceedingly adorned ladies who held fly-whisks of yak-tail hair and served him from nearby;
— accompanied by Durdhara, Prahasta, and the rākṣasa Mahāpārśva, by counselors versed in the true principles of counsel, and by the minister Nikumbha;
— flanked by four rākṣasas intoxicated with their strength, surrounding the entire world like the four oceans;
— attended by counselors expert in the principles of statecraft and by others of auspicious intelligence, like the lord of gods attended by the gods;
Hanūmān beheld the lord of rākṣasas of immeasurable splendor, stationed there like a rain-laden cloud resting on the peak of Meru.
Though being pressed upon by those rākṣasas of terrible valor, he was filled with supreme amazement and gazed upon the lord of rākṣasas.
Then Hanūmān, seeing the lord of rākṣasas blazing in splendor, reflected in his mind, overwhelmed by his brilliance.
"Ah, what form! Ah, what steadiness! Ah, what vigor! Ah, what radiance! Ah, how the king of rākṣasas is endowed with every auspicious mark!"
"Were adharma not so powerful in him, this lord of rākṣasas would be the protector of the world of gods together with Indra himself."
"It is indeed from this one that the worlds tremble — gods, asuras, and all — for this one, when enraged, has the capacity to reduce the world to a single ocean."
Thus the wise monkey turned over many such thoughts in his mind, having seen the power of the king of rākṣasas of immeasurable might.
Gazing up at him — that tawny-eyed one standing before him — Rāvaṇa the terror of the world, the long-armed one, was seized with great wrath.
